  "strategy": "# Approach and Rationale\nThe goal is to find a sequence of non-negative numbers that minimizes the score function score(a) = (2*n*max(convolve(a,a))) / (sum(a))^2. We are to do this within a 1500-second time budget without exceeding hyperparameter bounds based on the previous program's performance.\n\nI will design an improved approach using successive zeros insertion (SZI) iterations with normalization and improved optimization methods. This is fundamentally different from the previous program's multi-start approach; SZI more directly addresses the minimax problem by:\n1. Starting from a candidate sequence\n2. Iteratively shifting its length to n+1 by inserting a zero at a position that minimizes the score\n3. Using projected gradient descent to refine each new sequence\n4. Nesting the optimizer within the search function to avoid premature termination\n\nThis approach directly targets the dynamic structure of the search space better and ensures more consistent exploration.\n\n# Mathematical Specification\nWe represent a sequence as a vector a \\in \\mathbb{R}^n(x) with each element being non-negative. The score is defined as:\nscore(a) = (2 * n * max(|\\text{linear convolution}|)) / (sum(a))^2\n\nThe optimization is done in an adaptive way using projected gradient descent. For each candidate, we:\n- Compute the gradient by approximating the directional derivative\n- Project onto the non-negative domain\n- Clip excessively large values (up to 1000) as defined by the evaluator\n\n# Implementation Procedure\ndefpropose_candidate(seed=42,budget_s=1500,**kwargs):\n    \"\"\"Searches for the best sequence of non-negative numbers using successive zeros insertion. Returns the best found sequence.\n    \n    The approach iteratively extends the sequence by inserting zeros at strategic positions, then optimizes with projected gradient descent.\n    \"\"\"\n    importnumpyasnp\n    importtime\n    fromscipy.optimizeimportminimize\n    fromyour_projectutilitie